Cancel a pending soft reset when a save state is loaded

The Mega Drive's soft reset is not immediate. `soft_reset` (genesis.c) picks a
random moment within the next frame, stores it in `gen->reset_cycle` as a 68000
cycle count, and the reset happens when the CPU reaches that cycle. That field
is not part of a save state, and `genesis_deserialize` leaves it alone.

So a state loaded while a reset is pending is reset a moment later: the state
restores the 68000's cycle counter, the pending reset cycle (a count of the
timeline the state replaces) is already behind it or close ahead, and the first
frame after the load runs the reset vector. The player sees the game reboot
instead of the moment they saved.

Retro Pal meets this on every launch from a save: the app resets each core right
after loading a game (that is what reads the battery save on the other cores),
runs one frame, then loads the "Resume" or slot state. Measured with the 1.3.4
fixture (KłełeAtoms, Tools/md-bench path): load, reset, one frame, state load,
then 600 frames compared with the shipped core's own replay of that state:
0 of 600 pictures matched, and the frames were the game's boot screens. Without
the reset, 600 of 600. With this patch, the app's sequence matches too.

A reset requested before a load belongs to the timeline the load replaces, so
the load cancels it. RetroArch's frontend never resets just before loading a
state, which is why upstream does not meet this.

--- a/genesis.c
+++ b/genesis.c
@@ -257,6 +257,10 @@
 	uint8_t tmss_old = gen->tmss;
 	uint8_t old_z80_clock_bit = gen->vdp->test_regs[1] & 1;
 	gen->tmss = 0xFF;
+	//a reset requested before the load belongs to the timeline the state replaces:
+	//reset_cycle is not part of the state, and it is a cycle count of that timeline
+	gen->reset_cycle = CYCLE_NEVER;
+	gen->reset_requested = 0;
 	while (buf->cur_pos < buf->size)
 	{
 		load_section(buf);
